\title{The Gumbel Law and Extreme-Value Convergence:\\
The Analytic Backbone of the Fyodorov--Hiary--Keating Conjecture}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-13}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The Fyodorov--Hiary--Keating (FHK) conjecture predicts the precise extreme-value
statistics of the Riemann zeta function on the critical line. Writing
$M_T = \max_{t \in [T,2T]} \log|\zeta(\tfrac12 + it)|$, the conjecture asserts that
the recentered maximum $M_T - \log\log T + \tfrac32 \log\log\log T$ converges in
distribution, as $T \to \infty$, to the sum of two independent Gumbel random
variables. The full statement for $\zeta$ is open. Here we develop, from first
principles and in complete rigor, the analytic backbone on which the conjecture
stands: the Gumbel distribution as a genuine probability law, its exact
max-stability, and the extreme-value limit theorem that makes the Gumbel law the
universal attractor of maxima. We prove that the standard Gumbel CDF
$G(x)=e^{-e^{-x}}$ is a valid cumulative distribution function with all required
regularity and boundary behavior; that its density $g(x)=e^{-x-e^{-x}}$ is
positive and integrates to $1$; that the max-stability identity
$G(x+\log n)^n = G(x)$ holds exactly; and that the recentered maximum of $n$
i.i.d.\ $\Exp(1)$ variables, with CDF $(1-e^{-x}/n)^n$, converges pointwise to
$G(x)$. We further lift these results to the two-parameter location--scale Gumbel
family $G_{\mu,\beta}(x)=e^{-e^{-(x-\mu)/\beta}}$, establishing all analogous
properties and the scaled max-stability $G_{\mu,\beta}(x+\beta\log n)^n =
G_{\mu,\beta}(x)$. We close with a discussion of how these building blocks
assemble toward the FHK prediction and a program of tractable next steps.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The extreme values of the zeta function}
The Riemann zeta function $\zeta(s)$ carries, in the location of its zeros, the
deepest arithmetic information about the distribution of prime numbers. On the
critical line $s = \tfrac12 + it$ its modulus oscillates in an intricate,
quasi-random fashion. A central question concerns the local maxima of this
modulus: over a window $[T, 2T]$, how large can $\log|\zeta(\tfrac12+it)|$ become?

Fyodorov, Hiary, and Keating (2012), building on an analogy between the zeta
function and the characteristic polynomials of random unitary matrices,
conjectured a complete answer. They predicted both the typical size of the
maximum,
\[
M_T = \max_{t \in [T,2T]} \log|\zeta(\tfrac12+it)|
   \approx \log\log T - \tfrac32\log\log\log T,
\]
and the limiting law of its fluctuations: the recentered variable
$M_T - \log\log T + \tfrac32\log\log\log T$ converges in distribution to a sum of
two independent Gumbel random variables. Leading-order results were established
by Arguin, Belius, and Harper (2017); the full conjecture remains open.

\subsection{Log-correlated structure}
The conjecture rests on the recognition that $\log|\zeta(\tfrac12+it)|$ behaves
like a \emph{log-correlated random field}, whose values at $t, t'$ have covariance
decaying like $-\log|t-t'|$. Such fields possess an approximate hierarchical
correlation structure, and their extreme values are governed by the theory of
branching random walks and log-correlated Gaussian fields. That theory produces
the $-\tfrac32\log\log\log T$ correction and a limiting fluctuation that
decomposes into two independent Gumbel contributions.

\subsection{Scope}
The full FHK statement lies beyond current reach. Our aim is to lay, rigorously
and self-containedly, the analytic foundation on which it rests. All results
below are elementary real analysis, but together they demonstrate that the
statistical target of the conjecture is completely well-founded.

\section{The Gumbel distribution}

\begin{definition}[Gumbel CDF]
The standard Gumbel cumulative distribution function is
$G(x) = \exp(-e^{-x})$, $x \in \R$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Gumbel PDF]
The standard Gumbel probability density function is
$g(x) = \exp(-x - e^{-x})$, $x \in \R$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Positivity]
For all $x \in \R$, $G(x) > 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$G(x)$ is the exponential of a real number, hence strictly positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Upper bound]
For all $x \in \R$, $G(x) < 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $e^{-x} > 0$ we have $-e^{-x} < 0$, and $e^{y} < 1$ iff $y < 0$; hence
$G(x) = e^{-e^{-x}} < 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict monotonicity]
$G$ is strictly increasing on $\R$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $a < b$ then $-a > -b$, so $e^{-a} > e^{-b}$, hence $-e^{-a} < -e^{-b}$, and
applying the exponential gives $G(a) < G(b)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Continuity]
$G$ is continuous on $\R$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$G$ is a composition of continuous functions.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Boundary behavior]
$\displaystyle\lim_{x \to +\infty} G(x) = 1$ and
$\displaystyle\lim_{x \to -\infty} G(x) = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
As $x \to +\infty$, $e^{-x} \to 0$, so $G(x) \to e^0 = 1$. As $x \to -\infty$,
$e^{-x} \to +\infty$, so $-e^{-x} \to -\infty$ and $G(x) \to 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Median]
$G(-\log(\log 2)) = \tfrac12$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
With $x = -\log(\log 2)$ we have $e^{-x} = \log 2$, so
$G(x) = e^{-\log 2} = 2^{-1} = \tfrac12$. Here $\log 2 > 0$, so the logarithms are
well-defined.
\end{proof}

\section{Max-stability: why Gumbel is universal}

If $X_1,\dots,X_n$ are i.i.d.\ with CDF $F$, then $\max_i X_i$ has CDF $F^n$,
because $\{\max_i X_i \le x\} = \bigcap_i \{X_i \le x\}$ and independence turns the
intersection into a product. The Gumbel law is a fixed point of this operation, up
to a deterministic shift.

\begin{theorem}[Max-stability of the Gumbel law]
For every integer $n \ge 1$ and every $x \in \R$,
$G(x + \log n)^n = G(x)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Using $G(y) = e^{-e^{-y}}$ and $(e^{a})^n = e^{na}$,
\[
G(x+\log n)^n = \exp\!\bigl(-n\,e^{-(x+\log n)}\bigr)
             = \exp\!\bigl(-n\,e^{-x}\cdot\tfrac1n\bigr)
             = \exp(-e^{-x}) = G(x),
\]
where $e^{-\log n} = 1/n$ for $n \ge 1$.
\end{proof}

The maximum of $n$ independent Gumbel variables, after subtracting the
deterministic shift $\log n$, is exactly Gumbel again. This self-similarity is the
structural reason the Gumbel law is the universal attractor for maxima.

\section{Extreme-value convergence}

Let $X_1,\dots,X_n$ be i.i.d.\ $\Exp(1)$, so $\Pr[X_i \le y] = 1 - e^{-y}$ for
$y \ge 0$. The maximum has CDF $(1 - e^{-y})^n$; recentering by $\log n$ gives
\[
\Pr\!\bigl[\max_i X_i - \log n \le x\bigr]
 = \bigl(1 - e^{-(x+\log n)}\bigr)^n = \Bigl(1 - \tfrac{e^{-x}}{n}\Bigr)^n.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Convergence to the Gumbel law]
For every fixed $x \in \R$,
$\displaystyle\lim_{n \to \infty} \Bigl(1 - \tfrac{e^{-x}}{n}\Bigr)^n
 = G(x) = e^{-e^{-x}}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
This is the classical limit $(1 + a/n)^n \to e^{a}$ with $a = -e^{-x}$.
\end{proof}

The recentered maximum of $n$ i.i.d.\ exponentials converges in distribution to
the standard Gumbel law: the cleanest exact instance of the extreme-value
phenomenon underlying the FHK conjecture.

\section{The density and its normalization}

\begin{theorem}[Positivity of the density]
For all $x$, $g(x) > 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$g(x)$ is an exponential, hence positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$g$ is the derivative of $G$]
For every $x \in \R$, $G'(x) = g(x)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $G(x) = e^{u(x)}$ with $u(x) = -e^{-x}$, so $u'(x) = e^{-x}$. By the chain
rule $G'(x) = e^{u(x)}u'(x) = e^{-e^{-x}}e^{-x} = e^{-x-e^{-x}} = g(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Integrability and normalization]
$g$ is integrable over $\R$ and $\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} g(x)\,dx = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the substitution $u = e^{-x}$ the integral becomes $\int_0^\infty e^{-u}\,du =
1$. Equivalently, by the fundamental theorem of calculus applied to the
antiderivative $G$ together with the boundary limits,
$\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} g = \lim_{x\to+\infty} G(x) - \lim_{x\to-\infty} G(x)
= 1 - 0 = 1$.
\end{proof}

\section{The location--scale Gumbel family}

\begin{definition}[Location--scale Gumbel CDF]
For $\mu \in \R$ and $\beta > 0$,
$G_{\mu,\beta}(x) = \exp\!\bigl(-e^{-(x-\mu)/\beta}\bigr)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Reduction to the standard law]
$G_{\mu,\beta}(x) = G\bigl((x-\mu)/\beta\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from the definitions.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Basic properties]
For all $\mu\in\R$, $\beta>0$, $x\in\R$: (1) $G_{\mu,\beta}(x) > 0$;
(2) $G_{\mu,\beta}(x) < 1$; (3) $G_{\mu,\beta}$ is strictly increasing;
(4) $G_{\mu,\beta}$ is continuous.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Parts (1), (2), (4) follow from the corresponding standard-law results via the
reduction identity, since $x \mapsto (x-\mu)/\beta$ is continuous. For (3), this
map is strictly increasing when $\beta > 0$, and composing with the strictly
increasing $G$ preserves strict monotonicity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Scaled max-stability]
For $\beta > 0$, every integer $n \ge 1$, and every $x$,
$G_{\mu,\beta}(x + \beta\log n)^n = G_{\mu,\beta}(x)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the reduction identity,
$G_{\mu,\beta}(x + \beta\log n) = G\bigl((x-\mu)/\beta + \log n\bigr)$. Applying
standard max-stability with argument $(x-\mu)/\beta$ gives
$G_{\mu,\beta}(x+\beta\log n)^n = G\bigl((x-\mu)/\beta\bigr) = G_{\mu,\beta}(x)$.
\end{proof}

The scale $\beta$ rescales the recentering shift from $\log n$ to $\beta\log n$;
the invariance is otherwise identical.

\section{From the backbone to the FHK conjecture}

The results above assemble into the conceptual scaffolding of the FHK prediction.
First, the limiting object is a real probability law: the Gumbel law has a
positive density integrating to $1$, so the sum of two independent copies is a
genuine law with density given by the convolution $g \star g$. Second, Gumbel is
the correct universal target: it is the unique fixed point of the
max-and-recenter operation and arises as the exact limit of recentered maxima.
Third, the location--scale family provides the two-parameter flexibility needed
to match a field's intrinsic centering and spread; the $\log\log T$ centering and
the $\beta$-scaled shift are the analytic shadows of $\mu$ and $\beta$.

The remaining hard step---proving that $\log|\zeta(\tfrac12+it)|$ behaves like a
log-correlated field with the requisite precision, so that the
$-\tfrac32\log\log\log T$ correction and the two-fold Gumbel convolution
emerge---requires deep input from analytic number theory and is open.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The Gumbel distribution first arose as the law of record floods and material
failures; its reappearance at the summit of the Riemann zeta function is a vivid
instance of the universality of extreme-value statistics. Tractable extensions
that build directly on these results include: generalizing the convergence
theorem to any CDF in the Gumbel domain of attraction; the Gaussian case with the
classical normalizing constants $b_n = \sqrt{2\log n}$,
$a_n = b_n - (\log\log n + \log 4\pi)/(2b_n)$; the quantile function
$G^{-1}(p) = -\log(-\log p)$, the mean $\gamma$, and variance $\pi^2/6$; the
convolution density of the sum of two independent Gumbels; and a hierarchical
Gaussian model realizing the $\log N - \tfrac32\log\log N$ centering.

